  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote `src/main.ts` with the whole island scene, and `typecheck` passes. I couldn't run `vite build` or load it in a browser, so none of it has been looked at on screen. In particular, the custom GLSL shaders and the runtime `setLight` calls are untested. If a shader fails to compile, that draw is skipped silently.\n\n- **Island:** the terrain is one custom flat-shaded mesh with a face normal and colour per triangle. It has a sand beach, grass, rock and a pale summit. Scattered on it are about 16 low-poly pines and 9 rocks, all built in code.\n- **Water:** a 240×240 grid with a vertex-wave shader and faceted normals. It adds a Fresnel reflection of the sky, a sun/moon glint and animated foam along the shore. It fades into the horizon colour in the distance.\n- **Sky:** a large dome with its own shader (gradient, sunset glow, sun disc, moon, stars). I wrote this myself instead of using `sky.preetham`, because the docs say `app.atmosphere.setSky` is only a stub, so I didn't trust it to change colours live. A full day/night cycle takes 20 seconds, and the first frames start in daylight.\n- **Lighting:** a sun light with shadows and a moon light both follow the cycle. The ambient hemisphere light never drops below 0.45 intensity, so the scene is never black.\n- **Camera:** an orbit camera sits above the water and frames the island, rotating slowly at 0.12 rad/s.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after two animation frames following `app.ready()`.\n\nIf the engine ignores `setLight`, the lights keep their starting values. The scene still renders and is lit by the moon and ambient light at night, but the sun's intensity and colour won't change.\n\n",
